// The first in-language derived artifact (docs/metaprogramming/in-language-map-design.md,
// issues/in-language-map.md): the vector `map` template, written at layer m
// as a `meta fn` and evaluated by the elaborator wherever `.map` elaborates
// with this file in scope. The Rust `vec::map` schema (crates/elab/src/vec.rs)
// is the oracle it is cross-checked against, up to conversion.
//
// The shape records the round's central finding: genericity lives *inside*
// the code (Π over `Type<0>`, ordinary MLTT at layer c) because a layer-p
// type can never appear inside layer-c code — so the template is closed, and
// the use site applies the spliced payload to its element-type codes, the
// function, the length, and the vector, as ordinary applications.
//
// The `for vec.map` clause is the registration seam
// (docs/metaprogramming/meta-def-registration-design.md): it, and not this declaration's name,
// is what routes `.map` here, and claiming the slot means *having its type* —
// checked by conversion when the declaration registers. Every payload binder
// is named because the names are the argument protocol: the use site hands
// over ingredients by name, and this signature — not the call site — fixes the
// order they are applied in.
//
// The elim itself is the same one vec.rs builds by hand: recursion on the
// length with the Π-over-the-vector motive (a vector of neutral length
// cannot be projected, so the vector is abstracted and re-applied). Where
// vec.rs spells fourteen `shift_local`s, this spells none — binders bind.
meta fn map_code(g: Ctx) for vec.map → Code(g |-
    (A: Type<0>) → (B: Type<0>) → (f: A → B) → (k: Nat) → (v: [A; k]) → [B; k]
) {
    code {
        fun (A: Type<0>) => fun (B: Type<0>) => fun (f: A → B) => fun (k: Nat) =>
            match k {
                Zero => fun (v: [A; 0]) => [],
                Succ(zk, ih) => fun (v: [A; Succ(zk)]) =>
                    cons(f(head(v)), ih(tail(v))),
            }
    }
}

// The second artifact of the migration (issues/quotation-stage-artifacts.md):
// the vector `fold` template, same elim skeleton as `map_code` with an extra
// initial value. Right fold, matching the Rust `vec::fold` oracle —
// `[a, b, c]` folds to `f a (f b (f c z))` — which is the direction that
// computes on a constructor form without threading an accumulator.
//
// Only the motive differs in kind from `map_code`'s: the result is the plain
// `B` rather than a vector, so the length index constrains the *domain* alone
// — and that is exactly where the parametric-rigidity note (design doc §7)
// stops holding. `map_code` is rigid: the length index pins every position, so
// the well-typed template at the generic type *is* map. Here it is not. The
// step arm has three inhabitants of `B` to hand (`z`, `ih(tail(v))`, and the
// intended `f(head(v), ih(tail(v)))`) and no index rules the wrong two out, so
// a well-typed `fold_code` can be the constant fold. Genericity buys less when
// the result is not indexed — which is why the mutated-arm control the map
// round found unwritable *is* writable here, and is written
// (crates/elab/tests/in_language_fold.rs).
meta fn fold_code(g: Ctx) for vec.fold → Code(g |-
    (A: Type<0>) → (B: Type<0>) → (z: B) → (f: A → B → B) → (k: Nat) → (v: [A; k]) → B
) {
    code {
        fun (A: Type<0>) => fun (B: Type<0>) => fun (z: B) =>
            fun (f: A → B → B) => fun (k: Nat) =>
                match k {
                    Zero => fun (v: [A; 0]) => z,
                    Succ(zk, ih) => fun (v: [A; Succ(zk)]) =>
                        f(head(v), ih(tail(v))),
                }
    }
}

// The third artifact (issues/quotation-stage-artifacts.md), and the first that
// needed a new *surface* layer to be writable at all: `tabulate` turns an index
// function into data, so its payload is spelled with `Fin` and its indices
// (docs/language/surface-fin-indexing-design.md §9). This is where the Rust
// hand-shifting concentrated — `vec::tabulate` has to shift `B` by `d + 1` in
// the index function and by `d` in the domain, a distinction "invisible at a
// closed `B`" and guarded there by its own regression test
// (vec.rs:275-278). Here the binders do it: `B` is a binder, so no arithmetic
// exists to get wrong, and that d+1 subtlety cannot be spelled.
//
// The recursion is `map_code`'s, one level up: the length carries the motive
// and the *index function* is what the arms abstract, so the step arm feeds
// `f` the zero index and hands the tail's recursion the shifted one.
//
// **The design doc's rigidity prediction for this template is refuted.**
// §9 predicted `tabulate` would be rigid in `map`'s sense, on the grounds that
// its result `[B; k]` is length-indexed. The index *does* pin the shape — no
// arm can return a vector of the wrong length — but rigidity needs every
// *element* pinned too, and here it is not: the elements come out of `f`, and
// `f : Fin(Succ(zk)) → B` accepts `FZ` exactly where it accepts `FS(i)`.
// So the shift `Fin(zk) → Fin(Succ(zk))` is a choice parametricity does not
// make, and the constant-head tabulate — `ih(fun (i: Fin(zk)) => f(FZ))` —
// checks at this very signature and registers for this very slot
// (crates/elab/tests/in_language_tabulate.rs). The heuristic's real boundary is
// therefore narrower than "is the result indexed": `fold` lost rigidity by
// dropping the index, `tabulate` keeps the index and loses it anyway, because
// an indexed *container* of unconstrained elements constrains nothing about
// them. `map` stays rigid because its elements are `f`'s outputs at the only
// argument in scope; `tabulate`'s `f` takes an index it can be lied to about.
meta fn tabulate_code(g: Ctx) for vec.tabulate → Code(g |-
    (B: Type<0>) → (k: Nat) → (f: Fin(k) → B) → [B; k]
) {
    code {
        fun (B: Type<0>) => fun (k: Nat) =>
            match k {
                Zero => fun (f: Fin(0) → B) => [],
                Succ(zk, ih) => fun (f: Fin(Succ(zk)) → B) =>
                    cons(f(FZ), ih(fun (i: Fin(zk)) => f(FS(i)))),
            }
    }
}

// The fourth artifact, and the one whose oracle is kernel-side rather than
// `vec.rs`: `nth` is `heather_kernel::term::nth` (term.rs:1118), which
// `xs[i]` emits verbatim while no template claims the slot
// (surface_fin.rs:`indexing_emits_exactly_the_kernel_nth_schema` pins that as
// the left-hand side of this file's cross-check).
//
// The body is the design doc's §9 payload verbatim. Its `FS(j)` binder was
// unwritable when this landed — `j` was a reserved atom keyword
// ([[j-binder-parse-trap]]) — and was spelled `jx` here and in the tests until
// docs/language/let-and-blocks-design.md §5.2's rule 1 bought the name back.
//
// `Fin` has no eliminator of its own (docs/language/surface-fin-indexing-design.md
// §1.2), so the shape is forced from the outside in: recurse on the *length*,
// and the index becomes matchable inside each arm — `Fin(0)` is `Empty`, so the
// zero arm is the zero-arm `match i { }` (which is `elim_Empty`, and is the
// only thing that can be written there), and `Fin(Succ(zk))` is a sum, so the
// step arm cases.
//
// **The design doc's rigidity prediction for this template is also refuted**,
// and for a reason worth keeping. §9 conceded `nth` is not rigid — the result
// is the bare `A` — but predicted its step arm had "no wrong inhabitant to
// pick". It has one, and the `match` on the index is what puts it there: in the
// `FS` arm, `head(v) : A` is in scope beside `ih(tail(v), j) : A`, so
// `FS(j) => head(v)` — the constant-first-element projection — checks at this
// signature and registers for this slot
// (crates/elab/tests/in_language_nth.rs). Only the `FZ` arm is genuinely
// forced, and it is forced by *absence*: no `Fin(zk)` is in scope there, so the
// ih cannot be called at all and `head(v)` is the sole inhabitant. That is the
// pattern behind all four templates — an arm is forced when its context is too
// poor to offer an alternative, never because the result type is indexed — and
// three of four rounds have now found a writable mutant where §7's genericity
// heuristic predicted none. The conversion cross-check, not the type, is what
// carries adequacy here.
meta fn nth_code(g: Ctx) for vec.nth → Code(g |-
    (A: Type<0>) → (k: Nat) → (v: [A; k]) → (i: Fin(k)) → A
) {
    code {
        fun (A: Type<0>) => fun (k: Nat) =>
            match k {
                Zero => fun (v: [A; 0]) => fun (i: Fin(0)) => match i { },
                Succ(zk, ih) => fun (v: [A; Succ(zk)]) => fun (i: Fin(Succ(zk))) =>
                    match i {
                        FZ => head(v),
                        FS(j) => ih(tail(v), j),
                    },
            }
    }
}
